  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure will be in control of the region thru Sun night. \nThat means fair weather and seasonable temps are expected. \nTonight will be the coldest night. The northern valleys should \ndrop to or just below freezing, while some upper 30s may sneak \ndown to the coast and parts of southern NH. So I do expect some \nlocalized frost possible in the coldest spots for zones still \nactive in the growing season, but not widespread enough for \nheadlines. It will also be an ideal night for valley fog. I \nanticipate patchy fog possible just about everywhere, with the \nmajor valleys more likely to see dense fog. \n\nSun will see increasing clouds with the approach of the next \ntrof. They will gradually thicken and lower from the south and \neast Sun night. I cannot rule out a few showers sneaking into \nboth the mtns from the cold front itself and along the coast as \nmoisture is advected north into the approaching trof. Because of\nthe abundance of cloud cover, Sun night should be much warmer \nthan tonight and frost/fog is not expected.\n\nA front begins crossing the area Monday morning and some \nscattered showers may tag along with it, but the overall \nposition of the guidance hasn't budged. The latest NBM gives \njust about everyone around a 50 percent chance of seeing \nmeasurable rain, but any chances of over a tenth get relegated \nto the International Border and even then the probabilities are \nonly 50 to 70 percent. The ending time of the precipitation is \ntrending earlier as well, so it looks like any chance for a \nlight snow shower Monday night is going to be confined to the \nhigher elevations. A tightening pressure gradient Monday is \ngoing to bring west-northwesterly wind gusts into the 25- 35 mph\nrange. These gusts will be higher at higher elevations, so \nanyone planning a fall hike should prepare for wintry \nconditions. Winds remain elevated through Monday night, so \nradiational cooling won't be an issue, but the cold dry airmass \nbehind the front is still going to allow temperatures to fall \ninto the 30s area wide. This combined with the breezy winds \ncould bring windchills into the upper teens and 20s for the \nmountains and foothills. \n\n850 mb temperatures overhead Tuesday afternoon only look to end\nup in the -3 to 0C, so surface temperatures only mix into the \nupper 40s and low 50s in the north and mid to upper 50s in the \nsouth. Winds remain breezy on Tuesday, but are going to be \ntrending down as the day goes on as low pressure continues to \ndepart to the northeast. Tuesday may be the coldest night of the\nseason so far, and if winds do end up calming it certainly will\nbe. The latest NBM already brings near freezing temperatures \ndown into the coastal plain, and if it ends up looking like a \nfavorable radiational cooling night this area could see their \nfirst hard freeze. For now it looks like at least Frost \nAdvisories may be needed, but again much of the temperature \nforecast hinges on the wind activity. If we stay well mixed lows\nmay end up closer to what is observed on Monday night.\n\nHigh pressure settles over the Eastern Seaboard for the \nremainder of the week which will largely leave our area dry with\nnear normal temperatures. The exception will be in the \nmountains as troughing aloft may support upslope showers at \ntimes.",
